48
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,000
So probably the best place to start is what is OWAS?

49
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
What's the problem that we're trying to solve with it and with the Collaborator in particular?

50
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
So the best place to start with this is to look at how we traditionally look for security vulnerabilities within our applications.

51
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:52,000
So we have some target, we have a client and the client sends requests to the target.

52
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
We receive responses and then based on paydays we put in our requests and what we see in the response directly or in time information,

53
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
we then infer the presence of particular vulnerabilities.

54
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:11,000
So that's great. That obviously works really well for a large number of situations and people do that manually and automated.

55
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Scanners like Burp Scanner will also do the same thing.

56
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:19,000
However, there's another kind of category of vulnerabilities where this approach doesn't work for us.

57
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
So I'll call these Outer-Band or vulnerabilities.

58
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
So these are situations where there's any kind of asynchronousity.

59
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
That's one example here. So we send a request.

60
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
The tainted data is passed up to some external thread process or system.

61
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
The response is returned and then this payload is then processed unsafely by some system.

62
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,000
So obviously this approach of looking at the response won't work for this kind of situation.

63
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
And there are others. So stored issues, for example, where we're not aware of the retrieval points will be another one.

64
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:03,000
So there's a whole area of hidden attack surface where payloads within our requests potentially ending up triggering vulnerable behaviour.

65
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
But yet we have no visibility of this on the responses that our clients send them.

66
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:38,000
So we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to see that we're going to

67
