68
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:27,200
So this may or may not be the server interacting with directly with our client, we'll get the tank path, so we'll learn the insertion point. And then depending on the nature of the interaction, we learn more details from that. So for example, if it's an HTTP request, there may be a user agent, which then tells us more about either software stack or the server itself.

69
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:37,980
Okay, that all sounds great. But how can we go about triggering these network interactions and exposing these tank paths from systems within this, this hidden zone?

70
00:07:38,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Well, obviously, that's going to be heavily dependent on nature of the vulnerability. So how the vulnerable server is processing our data unsafely. But just to give you a few examples, we could put a domain name in a referrer header. So you can imagine this might be harvested by some system and then processed for analytical purposes.

71
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:12,000
If we've got some kind of asynchronous blind command injection, we might want to induce a DNS lookup using a payload like this, or fairly textbook, actually, kind of vulnerability.

72
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:26,000
So one of the things all these example players have in common is they all use a fully qualified domain name. And that's important because that will trigger a DNS lookup providing the domain isn't in a cache anywhere.

73
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:40,000
So building on that a little bit more, we want to ensure that obviously the domain we're going to use to trigger this network interaction isn't in a cache. And we also want to want to be able to unambiguously define the tank path.

74
00:08:40,000 --> 00:09:00,000
So apart from the data we're sending in, that's the interaction we receive on our server. So in order to do both of these things, we use one time use subdomains. So carry on with example of a referrer header, we might place a collaborator payload like this in the referrer header.

75
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:22,000
So this has this one time use subdomain. It's sent to some system, which then does some interaction with it. So we're going to trigger a DNS lookup to find out where this server is. This isn't a cache because there's a degree of randomness in this identifier.

76
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:39,000
So our DNS server is a little bit kind of special, really. So in addition to understanding the details of DNS protocol, it also recognises these one time use identifiers. And if it sees any of them, it caches the details of this interaction before

77
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:51,000
sorry, I'm too far, before responding with the IP address of the server itself. And this is key because it then means any subsequent network traffic then comes back to

78
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:05,000
our DNS server. So for example, if the referrer header has been used to do an HTTP GET, we'll send the initial request for the A or Quad A record for the IP address, and then we'll use the IP address to

79
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:14,000
make the HTTP request, and then the server can learn more details about the vulnerable server from the HTTP request.

80
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:29,000
So then a later date, Bert can come along and say, hey, do you know anything about this subdomain? And then assuming interactions occurred, we can then retrieve the details of that from our server. And this is really high level how the collaborator works.

81
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:44,000
So we're now in a position to say, what is the collaborator? Well, it is this magic server that I've just been talking about. So it sits there passively listening, never initiates any connections, it just listens for requests.

82
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:59,000
These requests come in two flavors, we have the ones from vulnerable servers, which tell us about interactions. So this collaborator has to understand DNS, because that's key to how this trick works.

83
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:03,000
So this is a lot of the work is to do. So this is a lot of the work that we're doing.

