So this is a lot of the work that we're doing.

96
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
We actually need an HTTP or SMTP request.

97
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
So these then come to the collaborator.

98
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
We recognize these one-time use identifiers.

99
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
We put them in our cache.

100
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Then the collaborator now has a second interface,

101
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:46,000
which is then used by BERT to come along and then retrieve the details of any interactions,

102
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
which have resulted from payloads that it generated.

103
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:54,000
This is really very much a conceptual view of the collaborator.

104
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
It's not amazingly similar from a private collaborator instance.

105
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
You run one of those.

106
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
So this is effectively what the collaborator is.

107
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:10,000
It's a stub DNS, HTTP and SMTP server, which recognizes these one-time use identifiers,

108
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:17,000
caches the resulting interactions, and then allows BERT to come along and retrieve the details

109
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and the interactions relating to payloads that it has generated.

110
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
Okay, so hopefully this all sounds great.

111
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
And you're thinking, how can I use BERT collaborator?

112
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,000
This sounds fantastic.

113
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:33,000
Well, the answer to that is if you're an existing pro or enterprise user,

114
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:36,000
then you almost certainly already are using the collaborator.

115
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:44,000
And that's because by default, the collaborator is used heavily by the scanner,

116
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,000
and by default, both the OS payloads are enabled.

117
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
So obviously you can configure this.

118
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:56,000
The scanner is highly configurable, so you can switch off the OS payloads if you have the need to do that.

119
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:59,000
The scanner also uses the public collaborator by default.

120
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:06,000
So this is an instance of the collaborator which we run at Portswigger for pro and enterprise users.

121
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
So again, if you need to, you can run your own private collaborator.

122
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:15,000
So for example, your systems might run a closed network, so you have to use your own instance,

123
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
and that's absolutely fine.

124
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:23,000
So there's no additional licensing cost around either using the public or the private collaborator.

125
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
If you have a BERT pro enterprise license, then you're good to go.

126
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,000
For reasons that are hopefully now apparent, for a private collaborator,

127
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:35,000
you will need a domain for which the collaborator server can be authoritative,

128
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
and you also need wildcard TLS certs.

129
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:44,000
Anywhere else support can help you with that if you require private collaborator set up.

130
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:50,000
So the collaborator was released in 2015, so we've had a long time to make use of it within the scanner,

131
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:56,000
and it's very heavily integrated into various scanning techniques.

132
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
