159
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:53,000
So this is actually an example I was talking earlier where we put a collaborator payload and referr header,

160
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:58,000
and that's then triggered an HTTP request to that subdomain.

161
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:03,000
This has actually come from a scan of our gin and juice shop, which is a intentionally vulnerable site,

162
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:09,000
which we run at Portswigger to allow people to familiarise themselves with BIRP or just to use in general.

163
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:14,000
So if that's of interest to you, then feel free to use it.

164
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:19,000
So with respect to issues, we always assign them both a severity and confidence.

165
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
So the severity here is shown as high.

166
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:26,000
And that's not true in general for all collaborator detected issues.

167
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:35,000
So the DNS counterpart of this would only be assigned informational severity because there's nothing inherently insecure about DNS lookup.

168
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:42,000
The confidence in this example is certain, and that's that is true for all collaborator detected issues.

169
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
And the reason for that is is twofold.

170
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:54,000
So a system has to have reached out to the collaborator and triggered DNS HTTP SMTP interaction for there to be an issue.

171
00:16:54,000 --> 00:17:03,000
And also due to the use of these one time use identifiers, we know for certain that it was this particular instance of BIRP, which triggered that interaction.

172
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:09,000
So what I'm getting at is the signal we get from the collaborator is really some of the strongest signaling we have for them.

173
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:13,000
The scanner and hence we report the confidence in certain.

174
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:23,000
So in addition to the many scan checks, which leverage the collaborator functionality, you can also access its capability by the extender extension API.

175
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:28,000
So this is both both the older BIRP extender callbacks API and the newer Montoya API.

176
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:32,000
The latter actually has slightly more functionality now, which is nice to see.

177
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:41,000
So many of our BATs in our BAT store leverage this capability so you can generate your own payloads and then pull from interactions.

178
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:45,000
And this is used in a variety of ways within these BATs.

179
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
So obviously you can use these as well.

180
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:52,000
And you can obviously write your own if you have some niche requirement.

181
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,000
This is one example of a BAT which uses the collaborator.

182
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:00,000
This is an extension which looks for the log shell vulnerability.

183
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
This is nice for two reasons really.

184
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:11,000
First of all, the collaborator was the perfect tool to detect this vulnerability and we saw a large increase in the load on our public collaborator server.

185
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:19,000
And in the number of requests for people wanting support in setting up their own private instances when the CVE went public.

186
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:23,000
And also this, like I said, was a community developed extension.

187
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:27,000
So it was in the BAT store within a couple of days of the CVE we made public.

188
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:32,000
