00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:27,000
Okay, folks.

232
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,000
So we'll just quickly go through some of the options around deployment and cover off the architecture for Enterprise.

233
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
And then we'll jump into the demo and show off some of the key features.

234
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:38,000
So Enterprise is made up of a number of core components.

235
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:44,000
The first of which is the web server. So this is how the users access the product itself.

236
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:47,000
But you can also access it by an API client if that's your preference.

237
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
Then we'll have the enterprise server.

238
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:52,000
This is essentially the business logic behind the scenes.

239
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:55,000
This is what controls the enterprise installation itself.

240
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:58,000
And then we'll have one or more scanning machines.

241
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:01,000
These are the entities that actually perform the scans.

242
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:04,000
Think of them as your automated penetration testers effectively.

243
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:08,000
And then each of those components will connect to some form of SQL database.

244
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
So we'll have a couple of different options.

245
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:14,000
In terms of your deployment options, probably our most popular is just the standard installer.

246
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:20,000
So you grab the release from our release page, deploy to an operating system of your choice.

247
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:23,000
So we support Windows, different flavors of Linux and Mac OS.

248
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:27,000
Essentially, if it's 64 bit and you can run Java, you should be okay.

249
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:34,000
And then we have the single machine deployment, which is just typical for POCs and trials, everything bundled into one physical or virtual machine.

250
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:39,000
And then a multi-system deployment, which is typical for a production deployment.

251
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:44,000
If your preference is around containerization, we also have the Kubernetes deployment available.

252
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
So we provide a helm chart for which to deploy the application.

253
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:51,000
And we're trying to be as agnostic as possible in terms of the underlying architecture.

254
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:55,000
So if you want to deploy to different cloud providers, that's certainly possible.

255
00:24:55,000 --> 00:25:00,000
And this gives you the benefit of the auto scaling scanning machines.

256
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,000
So with the standard installer, the architecture looks like this.

257
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:09,000
We'll have the web server and enterprise server live together on their own machine, again, physical or virtual.

258
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
You can go for our bundle database.

259
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:14,000
But again, that's only really typical for a trial or POC.

260
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:23,000
When installing to production, you would typically use your own external database, of which we support a number of different SQL databases.

261
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:26,000
And then we also have our scanning machine deployed separately.

262
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:30,000
So it's got dedicated resource because this is the entity that's actually performing the scans.

263
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:35,000
The scanning machine will talk to the enterprise server and the configured database.

264
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:38,000
And then we've got some outbound and inbound connections as well.

265
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:41,000
So we see that portswicker.net via port 443.

266
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:47,000
This is just for license activation and for how we deliver automatic updates if that's configured.

267
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:51,000
Of course, the scanning machine will want to talk to your target applications.

268
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:56,000
And by default, as Rick has just given a talk on, the PERC collaborator itself.

269
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:59,000
And that can be deployed privately if desired.

270
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:06,000
And then, as we mentioned already, the users or your API clients for connectivity into the system.

271
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:14,000
For Kubernetes, we do have some prerequisites that you'll need to meet in terms of the cluster that we're deploying the application to.

272
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:18,000
So this is going to be an ingress solution, typically a load balancer or ingress controller.

273
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
We'll also need a persistent volume claim.

274
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:24,000
So that's application dash PVC, whatever name you give it.

275
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:29,000
That needs to be shared by all the nodes in the namespace to which we're deploying enterprise.

276
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:36,000
And then for the auto scaling scanning machines, the cluster auto scaler must also be installed and enabled.

277
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:45,000
So rather than pre provisioning those scanning machines like we would with a standard installer, every time we initiate a scan job, a scan pod is created.

278
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:48,000
It'll run and then be killed off once the scan is finished.

279
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:53,000
So if you are deploying to a cloud environment, that's got some inherent benefits in terms of cost.

280
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:57,000
So based on those prerequisites, you should be able to deploy to anything you like.

281
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:03,000
AWS is your GCP or private cluster.

282
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:07,000
This is kind of the responsibilities for the for the community deployment split out.

283
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:10,000
So you'll see everything in the home chart on the right hand side.

284
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:18,000
You'll provide for the ingress that will talk to the web server service and that the database will sit outside that cluster as well.

285
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:27,000
But we do give you scripts for which to deploy the database with the appropriate users and provisions.

